**Protocols and standards (4 marks)**

A **protocol** is a set of agreed rules that governs how devices communicate: the format of the messages, the order of the exchanges, addressing, error detection and recovery. A **standard** is a specification agreed and published by an organisation (for example IEEE 802.3 for Ethernet, or the TCP/IP, HTTP, SMTP and DNS specifications). They are vital for the Internet because:

- **interoperability:** hardware and software made by different manufacturers, running on different operating systems in different countries, can understand each other and work together;
- **reliable and correct transfer of data:** rules for addressing and routing (IP), for splitting data into packets and putting them back in order, and for error checking and flow control (TCP) make sure that data reaches the right destination intact;
- **common services:** the web (HTTP/HTTPS), e-mail (SMTP, POP3, IMAP), file transfer (FTP) and names (DNS) are available to everybody in the same way, and security protocols (TLS) protect the exchanges;
- **growth and low cost:** new networks and devices can join the Internet by following the standards, and mass production of compatible equipment lowers the cost.
